same Go package: (1) `EXISTING IN-PACKAGE TEST FIXTURES MUST BE REUSED, NOT FORKED` — the canonical pair lives in `package httpapi` (not `httpapi_test`) deliberately so the existing newFakeIdempotencyStore, idemRef, recordingHandler, jsonHandler, postReq, run, decodeError, and idemPrincipal helpers from idempotency_test.go and middleware_test.go are reused verbatim. The closed-set bound on Claim/Complete/Release counters is part of the fakeIdempotencyStore contract and a forked helper would silently break that bound. Forward-applicable to every verification story whose subject is an HTTP middleware with an existing test corpus in the same package: extend, don't fork. The siblings BE-0396..BE-0404 should look for an existing `fake<Subject>Store` / `recording<Subject>` helper in the same package before adding a new one — the prior security-verification BE-0344+BE-0345 pattern said the same about handler-builders. (2) `CLOSED-SET SCENARIO TABLE MUST DOCUMENT THE EXCLUSION` — the replay closed set is success + 400/403/404 error envelopes; 5xx is deliberately excluded because the middleware releases the claim on 5xx (the contract is TestRequireIdempotencyServerErrorIsNotRecorded). Both the canonical pair's package-doc block AND the SECURITY.md section's Scope paragraph spell out the exclusion explicitly, so a future reader understands the asymmetry without re-reading the middleware. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose closed-set scenario table has a deliberate gap: name the gap in the section comment + SECURITY.md so a future contributor doesn't "fix" the apparent omission. (3) `BYTE-LITERAL RECORDED BODY, NOT STRUCTURAL JSON COMPARISON` — the runtime burst seeds a literal byte slice `{"schema_version":"yalla.output.v1","ok":true,...}` and asserts `got.body == string(recorded)`, not reflect.DeepEqual on json.Unmarshal'd maps. A structural comparison would mask a whitespace drift (an envelope re-rendered on replay would parse semantically equivalent but byte-different). Forward-applicable to every replay/recording invariant: assert on bytes, not parsed structures, when the contract is byte-identical determinism. Burst sizing: replayWorkers=4 × replayIterationsPerWorker=8 = 32 concurrent retries per run, enough to surface a non-deterministic recorder under -race without bloating CI wall-clock. The runtime burst observes per-iteration {status, body, header} and asserts post-burst aggregation in the main goroutine (no testing.T calls from workers — same shape as BE-0392's burst pattern).
- Verification-suite single-file multi-surface static-test, chaos-Postgres-disconnects instance (BE-0394 pattern, internal/release/verification_suite_chaos_postgres_disconnects_static_test.go + internal/controlplane/store/chaos_postgres_disconnects_test.go's new canonical pair TestChaosPostgresDisconnectsCoversCallSites + TestChaosPostgresDisconnectsMapsToTypedUnavailableEnvelope + new internal/controlplane/store/fakepg package (Server + DisconnectFault + NewServer) + SECURITY.md "## Chaos Tests For Postgres Disconnects" section AND a new row in the Required Verification Gates table + internal/release/AGENTS.md "## Verification suite: chaos tests for Postgres disconnects (BE-0394)" subsection; SIXTEENTH worked instance of the BE-0379..BE-0393 verification-suite mega-pattern, canonical command `go test -run TestChaosPostgresDisconnects ./...`, CI step name `Chaos tests for Postgres disconnects`, verify.sh prefix #19 inserted between BE-0393's #18 and the trailing optionals renumbered 19->20/20->21/21->22/22->23 in the same edit. Per-story knobs unchanged from BE-0393's bullet — (a) canonical command from PRD, (b) dedicated CI step name, (c) verify.sh numeric prefix (next number after last required step; renumber trailing optionals in same edit), (d) SECURITY.md section heading and substring set (BE-0394 substring count = 16; matches BE-0393's count), (e) AGENTS.md subsection mirroring SECURITY.md but speaking to maintainers. Load-bearing invariant unchanged: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. Three BE-0394-specific extensions to the chaos-classification template, all forward-applicable to ANY future chaos-Postgres / chaos-network-partition / chaos-rate-limit sibling whose subject is the store layer or any pgxpool-backed dependency: (1) `NEW IN-PROCESS FAKE NETWORK ENDPOINT FOR PGX` — unlike BE-0393's dokployfake (which exists as a long-standing HTTP test double under internal/controlplane/dokploy/dokployfake), there was no fakepg before this story. The minimal viable shape is a TCP listener on 127.0.0.1:0 that does NOT speak the Postgres wire protocol — it accepts the TCP connection (so pgx's dial completes successfully — important: graceful FIN, NOT RST via SetLinger(0), because RST races the dial on some kernels and surfaces as `connection reset by peer` on connect rather than the production failure mode of "dial succeeds, handshake fails") and then closes the conn, so pgx observes EOF on its startup-handshake read. The fake exposes a FIFO Fault queue (only DisconnectFault for now), a Host()/Port()/Addr() trio for DSN construction, an AcceptedConnections() counter so the harness can assert pgx made >=1 TCP attempt per op (a regression that short-circuited pgx without ever attempting a network connect would otherwise pass silently), Close() (idempotent), New(testing.TB) for tests inside the testing package, and NewServer() error for worker-goroutine constructors that cannot satisfy testing.TB. Forward-applicable to every future chaos-Postgres sibling: add SQLErrorFault (writes an ErrorResponse before closing — would require pgproto3 dependency from pgx), LatencyFault (delays the close), AuthFailFault (writes a FATAL ErrorResponse during startup). (2) `CLASSIFIER-WRAP CONTRACT, NOT END-TO-END CALLSITE WRAP` — unlike BE-0393's chaos test which exercises real production methods on the typed Dokploy client (c.GetDeployment, c.EnsureOrganization, c.RemoveService) so the test asserts on errors the client's PRODUCTION wrapping chokepoint already produced, BE-0394's chaos test exercises raw pgxpool ops (Pool.Ping, Pool.Acquire, Pool.Begin, Pool.Exec, Pool.Query) and EXPLICITLY wraps the resulting pgx error through apierr.StoreUnavailable inside the runtime member before asserting the contract. This is because the store layer does not currently route every call site through a single classifier chokepoint the way the Dokploy client does — store call sites return raw pgx errors and the wrapping happens at handler-shim boundaries. The chaos contract therefore pins the wrapper's behaviour given realistic pgx-derived errors, not every call site's wrapping behaviour. Forward-applicable to any verification story whose subject lacks a single production classifier chokepoint: assert the wrapper given realistic causes, not the production path. When a future story (BE-04xx) adds a store-layer classifier chokepoint, the chaos test can switch to invoking that chokepoint instead — the scenario table and the redaction/dependency/code assertions stay identical. (3) `ASYMMETRIC REDACTION CONTRACT — PASSWORD AT EVERY LEVEL, USERNAME AT WRAPPER ONLY` — pgx's connect error formatter intentionally includes the DSN username in error messages (for operator debuggability) but redacts the password. The chaos test's chaosPostgresSecretMarkers (cause-chain scan) therefore lists ONLY the password sentinel; the wrapper-envelope assertion (chaosPostgresEnvelopeMarkers) lists BOTH the password and the username sentinel because apierr.StoreUnavailable's rendered message is static ("the Yalla datastore is temporarily unavailable") and MUST NOT echo ANY pgx cause-chain content. The runtime member walks errors.Unwrap chain for the password marker AND asserts the wrapper envelope's rendered Error() string for both markers. Forward-applicable: any future chaos test whose subject is a wire-protocol library that includes debug info in errors should split the redaction contract into chain-level (real secrets only) + envelope-level (anything pgx-style libraries might leak into debug strings). The sentinel-literal approach (chaosPostgresSentinelPassword="yallachaossecret_DO_NOT_LEAK_42") keeps the assertion meaningful: if the wrapper EVER started embedding the cause's message, the sentinel would surface and the gate would trip on the first observation. Per-iteration setup cost is bounded by chaosPostgresConnectTimeout=750ms (per-attempt pgx connect deadline) which is strictly less than chaosPostgresOpTimeout=2s (per-op context deadline), so the burst (5 scenarios × 4 workers × 3 iterations = 60 iterations) completes in ~0.5s wall-clock.
