ks) so the analyser is robust to Markdown line wrap and prose changes around it. Forward-applicable to every opt-in verification story: add the entry to the Optional Tools subsection (creating it if needed), and pin only the canonical command literal in the static test, not the surrounding prose. (5) `SECURITY.md ROW's When COLUMN SAYS "Opt-in (`YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY=1`), nightly + manual", NOT THE UNIFORM "Every push and PR" USED BY EVERY OTHER GATE` — the When column is the operator-facing signal that distinguishes the opt-in cadence from the on-every-PR cadence. The static test pins the entire row literal so a regression that flipped the cadence to "Every push and PR" would fail the matcher; a regression that dropped the row entirely would also fail. The dedicated `## External Live-Dokploy Smoke Tests` section's 16 substring set includes a NEW invariant set distinct from BE-0399: the documented env vars (YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY, YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY_BASE_URL, YALLA_EXTERNAL_DOKPLOY_TOKEN), the cadence framing (opt-in, nightly), the canonical workflow path (.github/workflows/external-smoke.yml), the typed error code surface (yalla.error.v1), the redaction contract (redacted), the canonical function pair, and the self-locating static-test filename. Forward-applicable to every opt-in verification story: pin the When-column literal AND a dedicated section with env-var + cadence + workflow-path substrings. Load-bearing invariant unchanged from BE-0379..BE-0399: ONE FILE, SIX SURFACES, SELF-CHECK; drift on any surface fails ONE test, not six. ALSO: the smoke test's canonical pair uses a NON-CONTENTION shape (closed-set opt-in flag values + live-path-with-deterministic-redaction-branch) instead of the closed-set-coverage + per-decision-stability-burst shape of BE-0379..BE-0399 — appropriate for a SMOKE whose "live" branch is by definition external, where firing thousands of goroutines at a real Dokploy would be hostile to the upstream and meaningless under the race detector. Forward-applicable to every smoke / opt-in story whose live path is external infrastructure: drop the contention burst, replace it with a deterministic redaction-contract branch (httptest.NewServer that echoes a sentinel token; assert the typed wire error AND the absence of the token in the error chain) so the redaction property is enforced on every push and PR even when the live path is skipped. ALSO: the Dokploy client maps a 401/403 from the upstream to `apierr.Internal()` → `yerr.CodeInternal` (Yalla's OWN credentials rejected = internal Yalla misconfiguration, NOT customer-actionable auth failure) — pinning this in the synthetic-server branch is itself a load-bearing contract worth pinning across any future Dokploy-touching smoke.
ralph/progress.txt:180:- Cross-cutting capability package + at-rest encryption seam (BE-0339 pattern, internal/controlplane/secrets/{doc.go, provider.go, aesgcm.go, plaintext.go, *_test.go, AGENTS.md} + internal/controlplane/store/migrate/migrations/0029_organization_variables_encrypted_secrets.{up,down}.sql + internal/controlplane/store/organization_variable.go + organization_variable_service.go + organization_variable_secrets_at_rest_test.go + cmd/yalla-api/main.go buildSecretsProvider; structural template for BE-0340..0342 sibling at-rest stories (project / environment / service variable encryption-at-rest) and any future cross-cutting source-of-truth seam over a same-shape resource family — webhook signing secrets, deployment-config secrets, backup-target credentials, etc.). Six load-bearing rules for the cross-cutting package: (1) Land the package ONCE under internal/controlplane/<pkg>, with the Provider interface + a production implementation (AESGCM) + a tests-only passthrough implementation (Plaintext, restricted to local/test profiles by cmd/yalla-api) + a package AGENTS.md that documents BOTH the runtime contract AND the forward-applicable storage pattern (column shape, CHECK constraint shape, repository tuple shape, service-layer seam location). (2) Typed value-free errors (ErrInvalidCiphertext / ErrUnknownKey / ErrUnsupportedProvider) — the secrets package contract never echoes any of {plaintext, ciphertext bytes, key material, key id, nonce} into an error message; the only observable on a failure is the classification. (3) AES-256-GCM with per-Seal random crypto/rand nonce + AEAD-authenticated decryption + multi-key rotation pool (first key is active for Seal, all keys accepted for Open). KeyID derivation = first 8 bytes of SHA-256(key) hex-encoded (16 chars): deterministic across processes, non-secret (one-way hash), short enough for audit metadata. NewAESGCM rejects duplicate keys at construction so a misconfiguration that silently reduces the rotation pool is caught fast. (4) cmd/yalla-api wires the provider in ONE buildSecretsProvider helper near runStartupChecks, NOT one per scope. Strict profiles (staging/production) refuse to start without YALLA_SECRET_KEYS (config.requireStrictFields enforces) via an explicit sentinel error; permissive profiles (local/test) fall back to Plaintext with a Warn log line. (5) The interface is INTENTIONALLY same-width every scope needs (org/project/env/service variables), so a single provider configured at startup covers every variables table — sibling at-rest stories become mechanical clones of schema + service + test changes. (6) For internal-only read paths that legitimately need the plaintext (the future Dokploy renderer worker, BE-0343 variable-inheritance resolver), Open the sealed tuple through the same Provider passed into the service — NEVER a wire-facing endpoint. Six load-bearing rules for the storage shape per table: (1) Add three nullable columns (secret_provider text NULL, secret_key_id text NULL, secret_ciphertext bytea NULL) ALONGSIDE the existing plain `value` column, never instead of it; force value='' for secret rows and cipher columns NULL for non-secret rows via ONE CHECK constraint named <table>_secret_columns_consistent that pins the invariant in both directions. The CHECK is the defence-in-depth backstop the service relies on — a bug that tries to write a plaintext secret rolls back as deterministic apierr.Conflict rather than silent corruption. (2) Mirror DO $$ guards in BOTH the up and the down migration that REFUSE to run while is_secret=true rows exist with the wrong shape — a re-application against a database that accumulated plaintext secrets fails loudly rather than silently leaving them behind, and a rollback against a database with sealed rows fails loudly rather than silently losing every secret value. (3) Add a partial index on (secret_provider, secret_key_id) WHERE is_secret=true so the future key-rotation worker (BE-0351) can locate rows referencing a retired key without a full-table scan. (4) The repository's Upsert + UpdateMutable signatures take a closed-set Input tuple (<Table>VariableUpsert with Value/IsSecret/SecretProvider/SecretKeyID/SecretCiphertext) — keeps the signature narrow as new secret fields land and centralises the "nullable columns when is_secret=false" projection in one helper (nullableSecretColumns). (5) The OrganizationVariable struct (and siblings) gain the three encryption-at-rest fields as INTERNAL-only — never on the wire — and the LogValue redaction MUST be updated in the SAME edit to redact BOTH the plaintext AND the new ciphertext to output.Sentinel; an unstale slog record that captures the struct cannot leak either. (6) scanRow projection routes through sql.NullString for the nullable text columns (pgx-scan into *string rejects NULL). Five load-bearing rules for the service-layer seam: (1) Seal at the SERVICE layer, never the repository — Seal is a side-effecting call that depends on a runtime Provider not a *Tx, calling Seal BEFORE opening the transaction shortens the SQL critical section, and the repository stays pure SQL. The cleanest helper is a one-line projector (sealVariableValue) that takes the customer's input shape (<Table>VariableReplace) and returns the repository's tuple shape (<Table>VariableUpsert) — non-secret items pass through untouched, secret items go through Provider.Seal. (2) The Service's constructor (NewOrganizationVariableService) takes Provider as a REQUIRED dependency rejected at construction if nil — a production process cannot accidentally start with the at-rest seam disabled. (3) Patch path for sealed rows has THREE post-state cases the service must enumerate explicitly: (a) caller supplied a new value → use it verbatim; (b) caller supplied only is_secret AND the current row is_secret → Open the current ciphertext via Provider.Open to recover the plaintext, then re-Seal under the active key (the only correct way to re-key a secret without surfacing the plaintext to the caller); (c) otherwise → use the current row's `value` column. Skipping case (b) would either lose the secret bytes on a re-seal or write a "previously-sealed plaintext" string into the value column. (4) The demotion path (is_secret=true→false) reuses case (b) to recover the plaintext, then writes it back to the value column with NULL encryption columns — the ONLY customer path that surfaces a previously-sealed plaintext, and only as a deliberate operator action. (5) Seal failures surface as apierr.Internal with a value-free wrapped cause — a sealing error is a server-side problem, never customer input, and the wrap never echoes the plaintext. Six load-bearing rules for the integration-test surface: (1) The integration test file lives next to the existing repository tests as <table>_secrets_at_rest_test.go, uses testutil.RequireMigratedDB (skips cleanly when YALLA_TEST_DATABASE_URL is unset so go test ./... stays green without Postgres), and wires a REAL AES-256-GCM provider (mustAESGCM helper that mints a fresh 32-byte key per test so parallel tests cannot bleed key ids across each other). (2) Five pinned tests per scope: SealsSecretValuesAtRest (the load-bearing contract — secret value reaches Postgres ONLY as AES-GCM ciphertext under the active key id, value column is "", on-disk bytea does not contain the plaintext substring, ciphertext round-trips through Provider.Open, audit row never carries any of {plaintext, key name, hex ciphertext, key id, provider id}); PatchRotatesCiphertextOnEveryWrite (a patch that flips is_secret only on a sealed row Opens-then-Seals under a fresh nonce so on-disk bytes change between writes); PatchDemotesSecretToPlaintext (is_secret=true→false with no new value recovers the plaintext via Open and writes to the value column with NULL encryption columns); SchemaRejectsPlaintextSecretWrite (three direct INSERT attempts that would land a plaintext-secret row each surface a constraint violation whose error message names the constraint); LogValueRedactsCiphertextAndPlaintext (a structured log record that captures the variable struct redacts BOTH plaintext AND ciphertext to output.Sentinel while exposing provider id + key id). (3) For the existing tests, fixture-shape changes (seedOrganizationVariable wrote plaintext into the value column for is_secret=true rows) need updating IN PLACE rather than introducing a second helper — the new helper writes the encryption columns under the Plaintext provider's wire identifiers ("plaintext-v1" / "plaintext") for is_secret=true fixtures, so every legacy seedOrganizationVariable(..., true) call site keeps working. (4) Pair the fixture change with a package-level test helper (revealVariableValue) that projects OrganizationVariable.Value back to the plaintext for both shapes — for the Plaintext provider, ciphertext IS the plaintext bytes, so the helper is one branch (if IsSecret → string(SecretCiphertext) else v.Value). Tests that previously asserted v.Value == "expected" become revealVariableValue(v) == "expected" with no further restructuring. (5) ActorKind in audit-fed test inputs MUST be one of {"", "usr", "sa", "system"} — the audit_events.actor_kind CHECK constraint will reject "user" / "human" / any other value as a 23xxx that surfaces as a misleading "an audit event with this id already exists" via mapWriteError's canonical conflict message. The actual diagnostic is the actor_kind CHECK, not duplicate id. (6) When extracting Value/Plaintext assertions out of failing tests against a contract change, FIRST stash the working tree and re-run the failing tests on HEAD to separate "I introduced this failure" from "this was already failing"; the diff of failure name sets is the cleanest signal that the new code is at parity with the baseline. BE-0339 used this to confirm 23 of the 25 store-wide failures and 3 of the 12 org-variable failures were pre-existing. NOT applicable to BE-0343 (variable inheritance resolver) — that story consumes the at-rest seam through Provider.Open against multiple scopes simultaneously and needs its own pattern around the merge order + per-scope Open call site. NOT applicable to BE-0351 (secret encryption key rotation) — that story consumes the partial index organization_variables_secret_routing_idx + the KeyIDs() / ActiveKeyID() surface to enumerate rows referencing a retired key and re-Seal them, but the rotation worker is a separate operational concern from the at-rest write path this pattern owns.
ralph/progress.txt:197:- Pure orchestrator / workflow package = reconcile-shaped vertical (BE-0033 pattern, internal/controlplane/migrateimport/{doc.go, AGENTS.md, types.go, importer.go, importer_test.go}, the second instance of this shape after internal/controlplane/reconcile): canonical shape for ANY workflow that ingests external state, classifies it, and writes through ports — Dokploy import, drift reconciliation, billing reconciliation, compliance scanning, lifecycle teardown, etc. Package layout: doc.go (boundary + plan/apply + dry-run + redaction sections), AGENTS.md (conventions), types.go (every port interface PLUS every value type those interfaces reference — Scanner/Repository ports, Snapshot/Existing*/Create*Input value types, OwnerAssignment, PlanItem and Plan with stable JSON tags because the Plan IS the dry-run yalla.output.v1 payload; ItemStatus + ItemReason as the closed classification surface), importer.go (Config with Scanner + Repository + Logger + Now + Redactor; New() returns apierr.Internal on a missing port; Plan(ctx, in) is pure and never writes; Apply(ctx, plan) is best-effort and collects per-item failures in Result.Failures with redacted error strings; classify*() helpers are unit-testable in isolation; wrap{Scanner,Repository}Err() preserves catalogued apierr.* codes and upgrades untyped errors to apierr.DokployUnavailable (scanner) / apierr.StoreUnavailable (repository) / apierr.Internal (defensive contract assertions); a resolvedParents map seeded with the org-root entry lets the apply loop resolve child-after-parent in a single pass through the hierarchy-ordered items). The test file houses an in-package fakeScanner + fakeRepo with concurrent-safe mutexes and call recording, and covers: construction port validation (one nil per case → CodeInternal); Plan input validation (every required field, every kind/format check → CodeInvalidInput with the named field recoverable via apierr.ViolationsOf); the dry-run-doesn't-query-Repository contract (no-assignment Plan never calls the repo); every classification Status × Reason combination as a positive case (StatusReady / StatusSkipDuplicate / StatusSkipMissingParent / StatusSkipUnsupported / StatusSkipAlreadyImported / StatusPendingOwner); Scanner failure surfaces as the right apierr code (DokployUnavailable → yerr.CodeServer, NOT CodeUnavailable — that's for Yalla-owned dependencies); Repository failure preserves catalogued codes; secrets redacted in error strings AND in log buffer (slog over a bytes.Buffer, output.NewRedactor(secret), assert secret never appears); Apply persists in hierarchy order (organization → project → environment → service, asserted by scanning the recorded calls slice for ordering invariants); Apply continues past per-item failures (inject failServiceByDokployID for one specific service create, prove sibling creates still succeeded); Apply ctx-cancellation propagates (errors.Is(err, context.Canceled)); Apply on no-ready-items is a valid no-op accounting Skipped count. The Plan JSON shape is PUBLIC compatibility contract before the customer-facing endpoint even exists — stable field names, no pointers in PlanItem, no embedded interfaces, no time.Time on the wire — because the eventual POST endpoint (BE-0289 et al) serialises it as-is. Canonical test fixture ID format: kind prefix + "_" + 26-char Crockford base32 lowercase suffix from "0123456789abcdefghjkmnpqrstvwxyz" (no i, l, o, u); use "org_01hz00000000000000000000xx" style (4-char prefix + 20 zeros + 2-char tag = 26) — the testutil factory's IDs are deliberately non-canonical for some tests and will fail strict ParseID. Slug derivation goes one-way: domain.NormalizeSlug(dokployName) for the Dokploy → Yalla direction (a NormalizeSlug rejection is itself a classification, NOT an error to propagate); domain.DokployName(label, yallaID) is the OPPOSITE direction and must not be reused here. supportedServiceType(svc) AND-s dokploy.ServiceType.Valid() with the database-requires-engine rule and collapses both failure modes into a single StatusSkipUnsupported reason — the operator's remediation is the same. Reconcile and migrateimport are siblings under this pattern; future "resource lifecycle worker", "drift admin reconciler", "billing reconciler" packages should reuse the package layout verbatim.
ralph/progress.txt:349:- Pure N-scope inheritance resolver (BE-0343 pattern, internal/controlplane/variables/{doc.go, resolver.go, resolver_test.go, AGENTS.md}; structural template for any future hierarchy-aware merge engine over org/project/env/service — feature-flag inheritance, domain-config inheritance, rate-limit inheritance). Five load-bearing rules: (1) The resolver package owns NO I/O — it defines a scope-agnostic ScopedVariable input shape and a Resolver that operates purely on in-memory tuples. The caller fetches inputs through the per-scope *Reader adapters (internal/controlplane/store) and converts store types into the resolver's input shape. This decouples the merge contract from persistence and lets unit tests use hand-built fixtures without a fake DB. (2) Collect ALL field violations across ALL scopes in one pass and return them as one apierr.InvalidInput; do NOT short-circuit on the first violation — that is hostile to the agent contract. Field paths anchor on <scope>.variables[<index>].<field> and NEVER echo the submitted value. (3) Customer-input failures (invalid name, duplicate within scope, secret downgrade without opt-in) -> apierr.FieldViolation -> apierr.InvalidInput (HTTP 400). Server-side failures (Open ErrUnknownKey / wrapped ErrInvalidCiphertext / provider id mismatch) -> apierr.Internal with a wrapped cause for server-side logging (HTTP 500). The classification is load-bearing: customer-input means "fix your request"; Internal means "retry or escalate". (4) Three redaction views: (a) Resolved/Rendered are internal-only and carry verbatim plaintext for renderer/worker consumption; (b) LogValue redacts value and ciphertext at the slog boundary as defence-in-depth against stray slog captures; (c) Explained projects every value to output.Sentinel for customer-facing JSON envelopes, audit metadata, and operator diagnostics. Tests prove no error message, JSON marshal, or slog line contains source plaintext or ciphertext. (5) Closed-set Options.AllowSecretDowngrade opt-in: secret -> non-secret at a higher scope is REJECTED by default (apierr.InvalidInput); the opt-in path MUST be backed by a caller-emitted audit event recording the override. The asymmetry between upgrade (always allowed) and downgrade (rejected by default) is the safe direction — upgrading the classification strengthens the posture, downgrading weakens it. Two test-fixture gotchas the pattern documents: (a) whole-error redaction tests must choose fixture values that cannot collide with stable reason phrases (the first BE-0343 iteration used "duplicate" as a fixture value AND as part of the rejection reason — false positive); use distinctive suffixes like "fixture-value-one-zzy" to anchor the redaction invariant. (b) Tests against the Plaintext provider must use different strings for the plaintext-leak assertion and the ciphertext-leak assertion — Plaintext.Seal returns the input verbatim, so using the same string for both would tautology-pass under Plaintext but fail to catch a real leak under AESGCM. NOT applicable to the dokploy renderer's mergeVariables (renderer.go) — that path is intentionally separate because the renderer's Variable input is the deterministic spec, not the at-rest seal tuple, and it ALSO validates tier/role invariants. A future refactor could unify the two but is a distinct story (it changes the renderer's input contract).
